steps.body:
To build a result across passes, declare an **accumulator** before the loop and update it inside:

```cpp
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; ++i) {
    sum += i;
}
std::cout << sum << '\n';  // 55
```

It must live outside the loop. Declared inside the body, it would be created fresh, reset to 0 on every pass, and gone after the loop.

Start a sum at 0 and a product at 1. A product starting at 0 stays 0 forever.

Products grow fast. `13!` already passes the `int` limit of about 2.1 billion, and signed overflow is undefined behavior. Use a `long long` accumulator (8 bytes, up to about 9.2 quintillion):

```cpp
long long product = 1;
for (int i = 1; i <= 20; ++i) {
    product *= i;
}
std::cout << product << '\n';  // 2432902008176640000
```

The multiplication happens in `long long` because `product` is one, so `i` gets widened first. Even `long long` has a limit: it holds 20! but not 21!.

To **count** matches, put an `if` inside the loop and add 1 only when it holds:

```cpp
int evens = 0;
for (int i = 1; i <= 10; ++i) {
    if (i % 2 == 0) {
        ++evens;
    }
}
std::cout << evens << '\n';  // 5
```

