#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int oddSum = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 99; i += 2) {
        oddSum += i;
    }
    std::cout << "Odd sum: " << oddSum << '\n';

    long long power = 1;
    for (int i = 0; i < 40; ++i) {
        power *= 2;
    }
    std::cout << "2^40 = " << power << '\n';

    int both = 0;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 200; ++i) {
        if (i % 3 == 0 && i % 5 == 0) {
            ++both;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "Divisible by 3 and 5: " << both << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
A loop can sit inside another loop. The inner loop runs completely for each pass of the outer one, which makes rows and columns easy:

```cpp
for (int row = 1; row <= 3; ++row) {
    for (int col = 1; col <= row; ++col) {
        std::cout << '#';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
}
```

Give each loop its own variable name. The inner bound can use the outer variable, as `col <= row` does here.

Two keywords steer a loop from inside its body:

- `continue` skips the rest of this pass and jumps to the update, then the next check.
- `break` leaves the loop at once.

```cpp
for (int i = 1; i <= 100; ++i) {
    if (i > 5) {
        break;
    }
    if (i % 2 == 0) {
        continue;
    }
    std::cout << i << ' ';
}
```

This prints `1 3 5`. The header allows 100 passes, but `break` stops the loop once `i` passes 5. Reach for `break` when the reason to stop shows up inside the body.

`break` only exits the **innermost** loop it is in. A `break` in the inner loop ends that row early, and the outer loop carries on with the next row.

